2,609,294,1124,408,90,5187,2555,532,59,2533,3830,279,2661,52970,11,582,614,279,2701,1849,315,37906,1447,59,9640,59,7265,90,5187,2555,92,264,609,293,24984,272,609,294,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,17,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,24,24984,220,18,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,1124,31610,1124,1318,90,437,92,1124,31610,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,264,609,293,24984,272,609,294,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,481,18,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,22,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,532,59,2533,1986,6696,601,279,2701,37906,1447,16,13,17767,17,64,481,293,284,220,24,59,340,17,13,17767,17,66,481,294,284,220,18,59,340,18,13,17767,64,481,220,18,65,284,220,22,59,340,19,13,17767,66,481,220,18,67,284,481,16,59,692,1654,11625,279,1156,1378,37906,369,17767,64,57758,323,17767,65,59,7731,3830,23606,320,16,1648,17767,65,284,220,17,64,481,220,24,59,340,3830,23606,320,18,1648,17767,65,284,1124,37018,90,64,481,220,22,15170,18,11035,692,24509,1095,279,1378,23393,369,17767,65,59,7731,59,9640,17,64,481,220,24,284,1124,37018,90,64,481,220,22,15170,18,532,59,2533,57251,6711,1526,553,220,18,1447,59,9640,21,64,481,220,17,22,284,264,481,220,22,198,59,2533,49,682,81,8595,3793,1447,59,9640,20,64,284,220,17,15,1124,6383,550,264,284,220,19,198,59,2533,3136,3696,10607,17767,64,284,220,19,57758,1182,1119,17767,65,284,220,17,64,481,220,24,59,7731,59,9640,65,284,220,17,7,19,8,481,220,24,284,220,23,481,220,24,284,481,16,198,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ takes $\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix},$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}.$  Find the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under $\\mathbf{M}.$  Express your answer in the form \"$y = mx + b$\".\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we first need to determine the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\). Given that \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) transforms \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can express \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) as:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFrom the given transformations, we have the following system of equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the following equations:\n\n1. \\(2a - b = 9\\)\n2. \\(2c - d = 3\\)\n3. \\(a - 3b = 7\\)\n4. \\(c - 3d = -1\\)\n\nWe solve the first two equations for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\nFrom equation (1): \\(b = 2a - 9\\)\nFrom equation (3): \\(b = \\frac{a - 7}{3}\\)\n\nEquating the two expressions for \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n2a - 9 = \\frac{a - 7}{3}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 3:\n\n\\[\n6a - 27 = a - 7\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms:\n\n\\[\n5a = 20 \\implies a = 4\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a = 4\\) back into \\(b = 2a - 9\\):\n\n\\[\nb = 2(4) - 9 = 8 - 9 = -1\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"a03513ba6bcdafdac10213fb0262e45ced0724ba295371720fae83437cec2a44"},"seconds":78.38291144371033,"seed":498470671888039813,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under the transformation by matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we first need to determine the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) itself. The matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) transforms \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This gives us the following system of equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFrom the first transformation, we have:\n\n\\[\n2a - b = 9 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\n2c - d = 3 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nFrom the second transformation, we have:\n\n\\[\na - 3b = 7 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\\[\nc - 3d = -1 \\quad \\text{(4)}\n\\]\n\nWe solve equations (1) and (3) for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). From equation (1):\n\n\\[\nb = 2a - 9\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(b\\) into equation (3):\n\n\\[\na - 3(2a - 9) = 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\na - 6a + 27 = 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-5a + 27 = 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-5a = -20\n\\]\n\n\\[\na = 4\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(a = 4\\) back into \\(b = 2a - 9\\):\n\n\\[\nb = 2(4) - 9 = 8 - 9 = -1\n\\]\n\nNow we solve equations (2) and (4) for \\(c\\) and \\(d\\). From equation (2):\n\n\\[\nd = 2c - 3\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(d\\) into equation (4):\n\n\\[\nc - 3(2c - 3) = -1\n\\]\n\n\\[\nc - 6c + 9 = -1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-5c + 9 = -1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-5c = -10\n\\]\n\n\\[\nc = 2\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(c = 2\\) back into \\(d = 2c - 3\\):\n\n\\[\nd = 2(2) - 3 = 4 - 3 = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo find the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we consider a point \\((x, y)\\) on the line and its image \\((x', y')\\). The point \\((x, y)\\) on the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) can be written as \\((x, 2x + 1)\\). The image of this point under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x' \\\\ y' \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(x' = 2x - 1\\) and \\(y' = 4x + 1\\). We express \\(x\\) in terms of \\(x'\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{x' + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(x\\) into the expression for \\(y'\\):\n\n\\[\ny' = 4 \\left( \\frac{x' + 1}{2} \\right) + 1 = 2(x' + 1) + 1 = 2x' + 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under the transformation by matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we first need to determine the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) itself. The matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) transforms \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This gives us the following system of equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFrom the first transformation, we have:\n\n\\[\n2a - b = 9 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\n2c - d = 3 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nFrom the second transformation, we have:\n\n\\[\na - 3b = 7 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\\[\nc - 3d = -1 \\quad \\text{(4)}\n\\]\n\nWe solve equations (1) and (3) for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). From equation (1):\n\n\\[\nb = 2a - 9\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(b\\) into equation (3):\n\n\\[\na - 3(2a - 9) = 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\na - 6a + 27 = 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-5a + 27 = 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-5a = -20\n\\]\n\n\\[\na = 4\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(a = 4\\) back into \\(b = 2a - 9\\):\n\n\\[\nb = 2(4) - 9 = 8 - 9 = -1\n\\]\n\nNow we solve equations (2) and (4) for \\(c\\) and \\(d\\). From equation (2):\n\n\\[\nd = 2c - 3\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(d\\) into equation (4):\n\n\\[\nc - 3(2c - 3) = -1\n\\]\n\n\\[\nc - 6c + 9 = -1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-5c + 9 = -1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-5c = -10\n\\]\n\n\\[\nc = 2\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(c = 2\\) back into \\(d = 2c - 3\\):\n\n\\[\nd = 2(2) - 3 = 4 - 3 = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo find the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we consider a point \\((x, y)\\) on the line and its image \\((x', y')\\). The point \\((x, y)\\) on the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) can be written as \\((x, 2x + 1)\\). The image of this point under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x' \\\\ y' \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(x' = 2x - 1\\) and \\(y' = 4x + 1\\). We express \\(x\\) in terms of \\(x'\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{x' + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(x\\) into the expression for \\(y'\\):\n\n\\[\ny' = 4 \\left( \\frac{x' + 1}{2} \\right) + 1 = 2(x' + 1) + 1 = 2x' + 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}\n\\]","id":"precalculus:243"}
